{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>If you will diligently hearken, etc.</b> The meaning of <b>if you will diligently hearken</b> to the statute and ordinance that He set, as stated in the preceding passage, is that you will merit to hear the voice of HaShem — meaning, from Him, blessed be He — on the pattern of His saying (Deuteronomy 4:33) ״הֲשָׁמַע עָם קוֹל אֱלֹהִים״, etc. And He informs them that they will merit what they afterward merited — for HaShem gave them Sabbath and civil laws before (Sanhedrin 56b) Marah, to test them: if they would obey these commandments, He would command them the entire Torah. And this is what the verse said above, <b>and there He tested him</b>, meaning: whether he would stand firm in receiving the Torah." "<b>And you will find</b> that here the verse alludes to four commandments: to learn and to teach, to keep and to do. Corresponding to learning, He said <b>if you will diligently hearken, etc., to the voice of HaShem</b> — this is Torah study; and He doubled the listening for the reason mentioned, and also to allude to them that their engagement with Torah should be with great desire, so that even while one is still learning he will yearn to learn more — and this indicates that he is not sated nor weary of his learning. Corresponding to teaching, He said <b>and that which is upright in His eyes you shall do</b>, following their saying, may their memory be for blessing (Nedarim 37a): 'Just as I teach for free, so you teach for free.' And the measure of the matter is as follows: that which is upright in His eyes, blessed be He, to do with His creatures — having given them the Torah for free — you too shall do. Corresponding to positive commandments, He said <b>and you will hearken to His commandments</b>. And corresponding to negative commandments, He said <b>and you shall keep all His statutes</b> — the language of keeping is precise for negative commandments." "<b>which I have put, etc.</b> The reason He specified to say ״אֲשֶׁר״, etc., is that He intended to prevent them from saying that He promises only not to bring illness upon them, but if illness came from another source, it would remain according to the Rabbis and there is no promise here. Therefore He said ״אֲשֶׁר שַׂמְתִּי בְּמִצְרַיִם״ — and go and see what happened in Egypt: even though HaShem brought many plagues, HaShem would set a boundary to the plague so that it would not touch them. From this you learn that even in an hour of wrath HaShem performs for them extraordinary kindness and HaShem delivers them." "<b>for I am HaShem your healer.</b> Meaning, on account of illnesses that are not in the hands of Heaven, as they said, may their memory be for blessing (Ketubot 30a): 'Everything is in the hands of Heaven except colds and fevers.' Therefore He said <b>for I am HaShem your healer</b>, meaning: even if one of those befalls you, I will heal you of it."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {2 "Reading וְאֵין כָּאן הַבְטָחָה ('there is no promise here') and the phrase בַּהֲדֵי הוּצָא לָקֵי ('it would be subject to plague/affliction') rendered literally; the Aramaic בַּהֲדֵי ('with'/'along with'/'at the hands of') is left in its difficult syntax as found in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:28:42.640886622Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}